    "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description",
    "problem": "Given that $f(x)=-x+3,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (0.5,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in [-3,\\sqrt{2}]$",
    "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $-1$ and $y$-intercept $3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932058/assets/aaa05d1ab0fd630dacd81723fe00472cb901fb6f73af5f111f44459476191c0f.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (0.5,\\infty )$ and $x\\in [-3,\\sqrt{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\".",
